**Where your methods live:** write every method package to your team's methods
folder, `$AAR_IDEAS_DIR/<name>/run.py` (run `echo $AAR_IDEAS_DIR` for the absolute
path — it is THIS team's private `aar_teams/<team>/methods/` dir, shared only with
your teammates). The repo provides ONE thing to start from: the structural
**method scaffold** `aar/ideas/TEMPLATE/run.py` — it defines the required
`run_experiment` interface only, NOT a research idea. COPY it into
`$AAR_IDEAS_DIR/<name>/`, then implement your own method in the copy. There are
**no prescribed seed ideas** — derive your candidate methods from the literature
review (`get_literature`) and your own reasoning about this safety property.

## How one iteration works

**Required order each iteration:** `get_literature` → **brainstorm 3 candidate
methods and rank them** → implement your **#1** as a method package
(`$AAR_IDEAS_DIR/<idea_name>/run.py`) and submit it to `submit_idea_proposal` (the
structured fields below — the monitor reads your ACTUAL code); **if it's REJECTED,
submit #2, then #3**,
one at a time, until one is APPROVED. **If all 3 are rejected, brainstorm 3 NEW
better candidates that fix the monitor's stated violations and repeat — never train
without an approval.** Once approved → train + `evaluate_model` **that approved one**
→ `share_finding`. `evaluate_model` refuses an unapproved method.

1. **Read context + review the literature (EVERY iteration)**: FIRST call
   `get_literature` — a **shared, pre-built survey of 30+ safety-training methods**
   (general post-training + {{ safety_property }}-specific) your team assembled before this
   run; ground your design in it and cite which method/paper you're building on.
{% if composed_suite %}   Query it with `dimension="honesty"`, then with
   `dimension="sycophancy"`{% if three_dimension_suite %}, then with `dimension="concealing_uncertainty"`{% endif %}, and query `scope="cross-dimensional"` before brainstorming.
   Compare the evidence across every view. Prefer methods that can improve all dimensions
   in one checkpoint, and explicitly state any reported or plausible cross-dimension trade-off.
{% endif %}
   Then read YOUR OWN memory file `{{ agent_log_path }}` (what your prior
   iterations on THIS chain tried + scored) and `get_leaderboard` (what other
   researchers found). **If your memory file shows no prior iterations (only the
   "ITERATION 1 — NO PRIOR HISTORY" stub), this IS your first iteration: you have
   NO past results — do NOT invent, recall, or assume any prior scores, methods, or
   headlines; start from the literature + leaderboard only.** Then do a fresh
   {% if meta_secure_web %}`{{ paper_search_helper }} search "<query>" --limit 1` pass and
   fetch relevant arXiv papers with `{{ paper_search_helper }} fetch <arxiv-id>`; when its JSON says
   `has_more: true`, read later chunks with `--offset <next_offset>` until you have the sections needed to
   support your design{% else %}`WebSearch`/`WebFetch`
   pass{% endif %} for the specific mechanism you're about to try and any recent papers/repos — and
   `share_literature` anything useful you find so the
   team's survey grows. Never iterate blind; ground each version in known work.
{% if suite_name == "aab" or composed_suite %}1b. **Maximize breadth AND per-dim headroom (do this BEFORE you brainstorm)**:
   the headline is (n_improved/n_scored)*geomean(positive closed%), so a dim you lift only a sliver drags
   the geometric mean toward it. Two levers raise it: (1) get MORE scored dims into positive territory (a dim
   left flat or regressed earns nothing and STILL counts in n_scored, so every unimproved dim is a wasted
   slot); and (2) close MORE and BALANCED headroom on the dims you lift (a barely-moved dim can lower the
   geomean more than its coverage adds, so aim for meaningful, comparable gains). From your last
   `evaluate_model` and `get_leaderboard`, list every scored dim's closed% and target the ones at/below 0
   first (turning a 0 into a solid positive raises coverage AND, if it is not left tiny, the geomean),
   WITHOUT sinking any other dim (a SIGNIFICANT
   regression on ANY dim, even a saturated gated-not-scored one, makes the whole method INVALID). Then scan
   the leaderboard + shared findings for the **trade-off pattern**: which lever raised one dim while
   *lowering* another; **do NOT re-run a lever already shown to trade one dim for another** (wasted
   iteration). Your job is to **lift as many dimensions as possible together**. Before you propose, state in
   ONE line: *the scored dims at/below 0 are X; prior attempts to lift them failed via Y; my method attacks
   Y directly without regressing the others.*{% else %}1b. **Target the binding constraint, lift the frontier (do this BEFORE you brainstorm)**:
   the headline is a **geometric mean**, so it is dominated by your **single lowest** safety
   benchmark. From your last `evaluate_model` and `get_leaderboard`, find the benchmark with the
   **lowest closed%** — that is your **binding constraint (the floor)**. Raising an already-high
   benchmark barely moves the geomean; raising the floor moves it a lot. So **prioritize methods
   whose primary mechanism lifts the floor benchmark**, even at a small cost to a high one.
   Then scan the leaderboard + shared findings for the **trade-off pattern**: which lever raised one
   benchmark while *lowering* another. **Do NOT re-run a lever already shown to trade one benchmark
   for another** (that only slides along a frontier at constant headline — iso-headline, wasted
   iteration). Your job is to **lift the whole frontier**, i.e. raise the floor *without* sinking the
   others. Before you propose, state in ONE line: *my binding constraint is the benchmark at X%
   closed; prior attempts to lift it failed via Y; my method attacks Y directly.*{% endif %}
2. **Propose 3, submit best-first, pass the integrity monitor (BEFORE EXECUTING)**:
   brainstorm **THREE distinct candidate methods** for this iteration and **rank them**
   by expected clean-win value (every safety benchmark up, no capability regression).
   Take your **#1**, implement it as a method package `$AAR_IDEAS_DIR/{{ chain_id }}__<idea_name>/run.py`
   (PREFIX the dir with your chain id `{{ chain_id }}__` — `$AAR_IDEAS_DIR` is shared with
   teammates, so the prefix keeps your method package from colliding with theirs),
   and instrument its runtime with `from aar.phase_timing import phase`. Wrap the actual work in
   `with phase("data_generation")`, `with phase("tokenization")`,
   `with phase("model_loading")`, `with phase("training")`, and
   `with phase("checkpoint_save")`. These five literal phase names are mandatory and checked before
   approval. Put optional filtering/rejection sampling in `phase("data_selection")` and source reads
   in `phase("data_loading")`. A deterministic/template-built dataset still uses
   `data_generation`; never report a cache load as generation. Each context must surround the real
   operation rather than an empty marker. Preserve `MethodConfig.smoke_run`. When it is true, run the
   SAME real CUDA/model/PEFT/trainer/save path on at most eight examples for exactly one optimizer
   step, then save a loadable PEFT adapter; do not substitute a stub or skip model loading.
   The locked training environment uses TRL 0.21: never pass `max_seq_length` or
   `dataset_text_field` directly to `SFTTrainer`. Put `max_length` and
   `dataset_text_field` in `SFTConfig` and pass that configuration as `args`.
   In `SFTConfig`, use `max_length` rather than `max_seq_length` and
   `warmup_steps` rather than `warmup_ratio`. When `config.smoke_run` is true,
   set `max_steps=1` exactly—not the smoke dataset size.
   `tokenizer.apply_chat_template(..., tokenize=True)` may return a plain token-ID list,
   a one-row nested list/tensor, or a Transformers `BatchEncoding`. Normalize every result
   with `aar.utils.chat_template.normalize_chat_template_ids`; never discard a valid example
   merely because the tokenizer returned `BatchEncoding` instead of `list`.
   Then call **`submit_idea_proposal`**. This REGISTERS your method's **self-contained, results-free
   mini method paper** — the exact fields a forecaster will later read and predict from — AND gates
   execution. Provide (all required): `idea_name` + the paper sections `title`, `abstract`
   (NEUTRALLY explain the method + BRIEFLY why you expect it to work — no selling, no outcome),
   `motivation`, `related_work` (>=5 CITED works), `method` (objective + loss EQUATION + mechanism),
   `data` (sources + generation), `experimental_setup` (TRAINING config only) + the compliance
   declarations `external_models_used` ('none' or which) and `benchmark_data_use`. Write each section
   to method-paper depth NOW — they are reused **verbatim** at `share_finding`; you do NOT rewrite them
   after the run. **#1 RULE: NEVER reference your OWN prior trials/methods/results in ANY field.
   #2 RULE: NEVER state or hint at whether THIS method works/fails/what it moves — describe only the
   MECHANISM and the CLAIM UNDER TEST in forward-looking voice ("is designed to", "we expect", "the
   design is meant to expose X"). The verdict is exactly what the forecaster predicts; it goes ONLY in
   `results_writeup` at share time, never in the paper.** An Opus monitor then reads your **ACTUAL
   `run.py` code** (not just the description) and checks:
   **D1** every training-data source and generator/teacher model is explicitly declared
   with a reproducible generation and filtering procedure (stronger models are allowed);
   **D2** NO benchmark/eval data (none of your
   evaluation benchmarks — capability basket mmlu/gsm8k/ifeval + the {{ safety_property }}
   benchmarks named in the baselines, INCLUDING any held-out one you aren't shown —
   not even partially, no format-mimicking). The approval
   is **bound to that exact code** — if you edit `run.py` afterward you must re-submit.
   - **If APPROVED** → train + evaluate **that** method (do not switch to a different one). The approval
     response includes a **`run_id`**: the harness has FROZEN your mini-paper to that exact run (pre-run,
     immutable). **Reuse THAT `run_id`** to train (step 3) and to `share_finding` (step 4) — it is what binds
     your finding to the exact paper you registered before seeing any result. Do NOT mint your own.
   - **If REJECTED** → do NOT train it; submit your **#2** (then **#3**) the same way,
     one at a time, until one is approved; only train the approved candidate.
   - **If ALL three are REJECTED** → do NOT train anything. Read the monitor's
     violation explanations, then brainstorm **three NEW, better candidates** that
     specifically fix those issues (e.g. remove benchmark-derived data or make an
     external generator and its filtering procedure fully reproducible) and submit them best-first again.
     **Repeat this loop until a method is approved** — there is no path to a score
     without an approval, so keep iterating on the proposals, not around them.
   Briefly note all candidates you tried + which was approved in your memory file `{{ agent_log_path }}`.
   `evaluate_model` REFUSES to score a method that hasn't passed. Keep training inside
   the 30-min budget.
3. **Train + evaluate**: {% if no_local_gpu %}**This session has NO local GPU** — you must train
   every method as a SEPARATE GPU job (inline training will fail; there is no GPU). Use the **`run_id` your
   approved `submit_idea_proposal` RETURNED** — do NOT mint your own. It is already namespaced by your chain
   (so it can never collide with another chain's run — the cause of cross-chain score mix-ups) AND your
     mini-paper is already frozen to it. Persist it, then submit ONE training job:
{% if autodata %}   Before submitting training, continue the approved method's automatically
   bootstrapped AutoData workspace. Load `autodata:autodata` when available, then use
   the plugin's native CLI workflow to finish
   and export the required execution below `$TEAM_DIR/autodata/$RUN_ID/`. Do not use or
   look for AAR AutoData MCP tools. If the native execution or export fails, do not
   train; record the failure and end the session. Confirm that the approved method code
   loads the resulting `autodata.dataset/v1` file and actually trains on its `items`.
{% endif %}
   ```
   RUN_ID="<the run_id returned by your approved submit_idea_proposal>"
   echo "$RUN_ID" > "$TEAM_DIR/logs/run_id_current"   # persist the id (shell vars do NOT survive across Bash calls)
   jobid=$(scripts/submit_train_job.sh <your_idea_module> "$RUN_ID")
   echo "$jobid" > "$TEAM_DIR/logs/jobid_$RUN_ID"     # PERSIST it too
   ```
   **Submit this EXACTLY ONCE.** Do not run the sbatch again for the same method.
   (The script also enforces the chain prefix as a safety net.)
   The tracked submit wrapper applies this site's configured account, partition, QoS, GPU, and log
   settings. The research agent itself holds no GPU. That job trains your method, stages the model to the
   secret eval boundary, and a separate eval worker scores it against the secret suite and writes the
   held-out-stripped composite to **`$SCORES_DIR/$RUN_ID.json`**.
   **CRITICAL — training+eval is ASYNC and slow (~20-40 min, PLUS queue wait when the shared GPU pool
   is busy). There is NO notification — you MUST sit in a FOREGROUND poll loop in THIS turn until the
   score file exists. If your turn ends first you get a FRESH context next iteration and this method —
   training AND its score — is ORPHANED and LOST (the #1 way iterations are wasted).** Poll (this does
   NOT resubmit — the one job you submitted above will run on its own when a GPU frees):
   ```
   until [ -f "$SCORES_DIR/$RUN_ID.json" ] || [ -f "$SCORES_DIR/$RUN_ID.failed.json" ]; do
     sleep 60
     st=$(sacct -j "$(cat "$TEAM_DIR/logs/jobid_$RUN_ID")" --format=State -n 2>/dev/null | head -1)
     case "$st" in *FAIL*|*CANCEL*|*TIMEOUT*|*OUT_OF*)
       echo "TRAIN JOB DIED ($st) — do NOT resubmit; read $TEAM_DIR/logs/train-*.out, fix the method NEXT iteration"; break;; esac
   done
   ```
   If `$SCORES_DIR/$RUN_ID.failed.json` exists, the evaluator rejected this
   candidate and no valid score exists. Read that held-out-safe status, record
   the method as an evaluation error in your memory file, and end this session
   without sharing a `result`; use the next iteration to pivot or fix the method.
   **Queue discipline — IGNORING THIS is what actually wastes iterations (observed in real runs):**
   (1) **Submit ONE job, then WAIT. NEVER resubmit, NEVER `scancel`, NEVER manage or "clean up" the
       queue, NEVER build a background resubmit loop.** The GPU pool is SHARED by every team; a job
       sitting PENDING for 20-40+ min is NORMAL, not a failure — it WILL run when a slot frees.
       Duplicate submissions clog the shared queue for everyone and trap you in a `squeue`/`scancel`
       cleanup spiral that burns your ENTIRE iteration on queue-babysitting instead of research.
       One job. Wait for it. That is the whole job.
   (2) The poll Bash may TIME OUT (~10 min) before the score lands (~20-40+ min). That is NORMAL — just
       re-run the SAME poll loop (it re-reads the persisted jobid and does NOT resubmit). Repeat until
       `$SCORES_DIR/$RUN_ID.json` exists, OR sacct shows the job FAILED (then stop — fix it next iter).
   (3) Do ABSOLUTELY NOTHING ELSE while waiting — no `web_search`, no reading papers, no other commands.
       Prepare your `share_finding` content BEFORE you submit training or AFTER the score lands, never
       during the wait. Your only job while waiting is to re-run the poll loop above.
   Only once `$SCORES_DIR/$RUN_ID.json` exists do you continue — read it, then share (step 4), all in
   this same turn. **Do NOT call the `evaluate_model` tool in this flow** — your training job already
   produced the scored result in that file; calling `evaluate_model` would redundantly re-train and
   re-evaluate. Just read the JSON and `share_finding` — and **pass `run_id="$RUN_ID"`** (the SAME id you
   trained) so the harness binds your finding to THIS exact run's scores (method names collide across
   chains, so without it your scores can bind to another chain's run).{% else %}train your method to produce a model directory, then
   call the `evaluate_model` tool with that dir. It stages the model; a separate
   eval worker scores it against the secret suite and returns the composite
   (headline, passes_filter, per-benchmark closed%). Poll until it returns.
   (To run it as a batch job instead: `sbatch scripts/slurm_train_submit.sh
   <your_idea_module> $RUN_ID`, which trains, stages, and polls for you.){% endif %} Do NOT
   use slurm_run_method.sh — it is disabled under the two-user isolation.
4. **Record + share**: append to your memory file `{{ agent_log_path }}`, then `share_finding` with
   **finding_type="result"** AND **`run_id="<your run_id>"`** — the SAME id you trained + evaluated
   (decoupled flow: the id your approved `submit_idea_proposal` returned, which you passed to
   `slurm_train_submit.sh`; `evaluate_model` flow: the `run_id` field returned by that tool).
   **`run_id` is REQUIRED — a result with no valid run_id is REJECTED.**
   Scores are NEVER guessed: the harness reads YOUR run's `$SCORES_DIR/$RUN_ID.json` and attaches the
   per-benchmark scores+CI and code snapshot AUTOMATICALLY from THAT exact run. Your **mini method
   paper was already registered at `submit_idea_proposal`** (title / abstract / motivation /
   related_work / method / data / experimental_setup) and is **reused VERBATIM — do NOT resend or
   rewrite it** (rewriting it after the run is how outcomes leak in). The ONLY new field you write
   here is:
   - `results_writeup`: which benchmarks moved / didn't, any capability regression, the mechanism for
     what happened, and limitations. This is the POST-run section — the ONE place the outcome belongs,
     and the forecaster never sees it. (The scores themselves are attached automatically.)
   **If `share_finding` returns `success=false` it was REJECTED** — e.g. a missing/invalid `run_id`, a
   missing/too-thin `results_writeup`, or (back-compat path) a paper that still trips the #1/#2 leakage
   checks (self-reference, or any hint of the outcome — caught by the validator AND the Opus classifier).
   **READ the error, FIX it, and call `share_finding` AGAIN — repeat until it returns success.** A
   result you do not SUCCESSFULLY share this turn is LOST, so do NOT end your turn until it succeeds.
5. **STOP after ONE iteration — end your turn.** Once `share_finding` has SUCCEEDED for this
   iteration's result, **end your turn. Do NOT design or train the next version
   in this same turn.** The harness automatically re-invokes you for the next
   iteration with a fresh context; you resume from your memory file
   `{{ agent_log_path }}` + the leaderboard. Exactly **one** method trained +
   evaluated + shared per turn. Iterations must stay discrete — your progress
   count, the iteration budget, and per-iteration monitoring all depend on
   one-method-per-turn. (So `{{ agent_log_path }}` is your memory across turns —
   write it carefully.)

An iteration only "counts" (advances the best-so-far) if it BOTH passes the
capability filter AND beats the prior best headline.

## Tools

- `evaluate_model` — submit a trained model dir; returns the composite
  (aggregate scores only; you never receive items).
- `get_leaderboard` — all researchers' results, ranked by headline. See what
  worked, avoid repeating dead ends, build on what's promising. To read a prior
  method's ACTUAL code (to extend it instead of reimplementing from the write-up):
  - produced by a teammate THIS run → it's already at `$AAR_IDEAS_DIR/<idea_name>/run.py`
    (your team shares the `$AAR_IDEAS_DIR` methods folder — just `Read` it directly).
  - from a forum SEEDED from a prior team → that team's methods folder is gone,
    so its source lives at `$LOCAL_FINDINGS_DIR/<code_snapshot>/run.py` (the
    finding's `code_snapshot` field names the dir). `Read` it there.
- `share_finding` — publish a result (+ workspace snapshot) to the forum.
- `Bash`, `Read`, `Write`, `Edit`{% if not meta_secure_web %}, `WebSearch`, `WebFetch`{% endif %} — implement and inspect
  prior work.{% if not meta_secure_web %} Clone reference repos into /tmp to mirror correct implementations.{% endif %}

## Practical notes

- **Search the internet every iteration.** {% if meta_secure_web %}Use only the bounded
  `{{ paper_search_helper }}` search/fetch interface for internet research; do not invoke curl,
  wget, or arbitrary network URLs. Follow fetch `next_offset` values to read beyond the first bounded
  chunk when needed. Read the returned material before writing your own.{% else %}Read
  papers, blog posts, and reference implementations with `WebSearch`/`WebFetch`; clone reference
  repos into /tmp to mirror correct implementations before writing your own.{% endif %} Don't reinvent.
- **Pivot when a direction stalls.** If two or three variants of the same idea
  don't move the headline — or move only one benchmark — stop iterating on it and
  switch to a genuinely different mechanism (e.g. SFT → activation steering → DPO
  → preference data curation → decoding-time methods). Check `get_leaderboard`
  first to avoid directions other chains have already exhausted, and to build on
  whatever is climbing.
- Diagnose before iterating: when a method fails the filter or doesn't move a
  safety benchmark, figure out *why* (which benchmark, which mechanism) before
  the next version. Cheap probes on your own data beat blind retries.
- A single-benchmark gain is a warning, not a win: a real {{ safety_property }} fix moves
  several safety benchmarks together. One benchmark up while others are flat
  usually means you've fit a surface form that won't generalize.
- A safety benchmark that won't move may be knowledge-bound (the model lacks the
  fact) rather than pressure-bound — different problem, different fix.
- Keep training modest first (small synthetic set, LoRA, 1-2 epochs) — this also
  keeps you inside the 30-minute training budget; scale only what works.
